EXT. HOT-AIR BALLOON BASKET - 2,000 FEET - SUNRISE

A wicker basket the size of a closet drifts over a patchwork of farmland. Gold light. Cows like grains of rice.

GERALDINE PLUMMER, sixties, floral scarf, holds a flask of champagne. Her husband PERCIVAL, sixties, straw hat, grips the burner cord with the stiff calm of a man who doesn't know what it does.

GERALDINE
It's lovely.

PERCIVAL
It is.

GERALDINE
Forty years, and you finally took me up.

PERCIVAL
Forty-one.

GERALDINE
Forty-one.

She pours two paper cups. They clink. They sip. Silence. The burner hisses.

PERCIVAL
Geraldine. There's something I should tell you.

GERALDINE
Oh?

PERCIVAL
The pilot didn't come.

GERALDINE
What do you mean?

PERCIVAL
The man at the field. He had a cold. He said, "You can manage."

GERALDINE
Percival.

PERCIVAL
I read the manual.

GERALDINE
Percival!

PERCIVAL
It's a very short manual.

The basket tilts. A gust. Geraldine grabs the rope.

GERALDINE
How do we land?

PERCIVAL
It says: "Gently."

PERCIVAL
Don't panic!

GERALDINE ^
I'm not panicking!

They both scream as the balloon lurches toward a barn.
